        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Is it normal for a treadmill to hum?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes. The motor, its cooling fan and the power electronics all hum in normal use, and on most home treadmills the pitch rises with belt speed. What matters is a change: a hum that strains when you step on, turns to grinding, or comes with a smell.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why does my treadmill hum but the belt will not move?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Something is stopping the drive or the motor cannot start. Common causes are an object caught under the belt, a seized roller, a broken or slipped drive belt, a very dry deck with an over-tight belt, or a motor, capacitor or controller fault. Pull the key and unplug at once, then check by hand.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why does my treadmill motor hum louder when I walk on it?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The motor is working harder under load, usually because of friction between the belt and deck. Lubricate as the manual says, check the belt is not over-tight, and clean under it. If that does not change it, the belt, deck or motor may be worn.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why is my treadmill buzzing when it is turned off?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              If it buzzes while switched off but still plugged in, the sound is coming from the power side: the power supply, the switch, the cord or the outlet. Unplug it and check the outlet and cord for heat or damage. A buzz from the wall outlet itself needs an electrician.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can an extension cord make a treadmill hum?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It can contribute. A long or thin cord adds resistance, which can leave the motor short of supply so it works harder and runs hotter. Most manuals say to plug directly into a grounded wall outlet, which also removes one variable while you diagnose.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why does my treadmill incline motor hum without moving?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually something under the deck, a floor or rug resisting the lift, or lost calibration. Clear the area, level the machine and run the incline calibration in your manual. If it still hums without moving, stop using the incline and contact support.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a humming treadmill motor going bad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not on its own. A steady hum is normal. Signs of a motor problem are a hum with grinding or crackle, a whine that keeps rising, surging speed, loss of power under load, or a motor that hums without turning.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

